; Hexlife Loop e06 - Hybrid Shifted (Negative Coordinates)
; Uses Axial Peano logic with shifted input to force negative coordinate usage.

(cell lives 2)

; === 0. Definitions ===
(peano 0 Z)
(peano 1 (S Z))
(peano 2 (S (S Z)))
(peano 3 (S (S (S Z))))
(peano 4 (S (S (S (S Z)))))
(peano -1 (P Z))
(peano -2 (P (P Z)))
(peano -3 (P (P (P Z))))

; === 1. Simulation Logic ===
(rule 0 (, (alive $q $r) (neighbor $q $r $nq $nr))
        (O (count (anbs $nq $nr $k) $k ($q $r))))

(rule 1 (, (alive $q $r)) (O (- (alive $q $r))))
(rule 2 (, (anbs $q $r $c) (cell lives $c)) (O (+ (alive $q $r))))
(rule 3 (, (anbs $q $r $c)) (O (- (anbs $q $r $c))))

; === 2. Setup ===
(step 0) (step 1) (step 2) (step 3) (step 4)
; (step 5) (step 6) (step 7) (step 8) (step 9)

; Shifted Input (-2)
; (2 1) -> (0 -1)
; (1 2) -> (-1 0)
; (1 3) -> (-1 1)
(input-alive 0 -1)
(input-alive -1 0)
(input-alive -1 1)

; === 3. Execution Pipeline ===
(exec (1 (0))
      (, (input-alive $q $r) (peano $q $pq) (peano $r $pr))
      (O (+ (alive $pq $pr)) (- (input-alive $q $r))))

(exec (2 (0))
      (, (step $s)
         (rule $phase $lhs $rhs))
      (, (exec (3 ($s ($phase (0)))) $lhs $rhs)))

(exec (4 (0))
      (, (alive $pq $pr) (peano $q $pq) (peano $r $pr))
      (O (+ (output-alive $q $r))))

; === 4. Neighbor Logic ===
(neighbor (P $q) $r $q $r)
(neighbor Z $r (S Z) $r)
(neighbor (S $q) $r (S (S $q)) $r)
(neighbor (P $q) (S $r) $q $r)
(neighbor (P $q) Z $q (P Z))
(neighbor (P $q) (P $r) $q (P (P $r)))
(neighbor Z (S $r) (S Z) $r)
(neighbor Z Z (S Z) (P Z))
(neighbor Z (P $r) (S Z) (P (P $r)))
(neighbor (S $q) (S $r) (S (S $q)) $r)
(neighbor (S $q) Z (S (S $q)) (P Z))
(neighbor (S $q) (P $r) (S (S $q)) (P (P $r)))
(neighbor $q (S $r) $q $r)
(neighbor $q Z $q (P Z))
(neighbor $q (P $r) $q (P (P $r)))
(neighbor (S $q) $r $q $r)
(neighbor Z $r (P Z) $r)
(neighbor (P $q) $r (P (P $q)) $r)
(neighbor (S $q) (P $r) $q $r)
(neighbor (S $q) Z $q (S Z))
(neighbor (S $q) (S $r) $q (S (S $r)))
(neighbor Z (P $r) (P Z) $r)
(neighbor Z Z (P Z) (S Z))
(neighbor Z (S $r) (P Z) (S (S $r)))
(neighbor (P $q) (P $r) (P (P $q)) $r)
(neighbor (P $q) Z (P (P $q)) (S Z))
(neighbor (P $q) (S $r) (P (P $q)) (S (S $r)))
(neighbor $q (P $r) $q $r)
(neighbor $q Z $q (S Z))
(neighbor $q (S $r) $q (S (S $r)))

;; Gemini 3 Pro Preview explanation:
; These 30 rules are a compiled state machine that performs vector addition on Peano numbers.

;   The Didactic Point
;   Instead of calculating q + 1 and r - 1 using general-purpose arithmetic, we have pre-calculated every possible structural transition that can happen to a Peano term when you move in one of the 6 hexagonal directions.

;   The "Compilation"
;   We are adding a direction vector (dq, dr) to a coordinate (q, r).
;   Since dq and dr are always -1, 0, or +1, we only need to handle three operations: Increment, Decrement, and Same.

;   The rules break down the cross-product of these operations on the structure of q and r.

;   1. The "Same" Operation (The easiest)
;   If a direction is (+1, 0), we change q but keep r exactly the same.
;    * Rule: (neighbor ... $r ... $r)
;    * Logic: "Whatever $r$ is, just copy it to the output."
;    * This is why you see simple variables like $r passing through unchanged in many rules.

;   2. The "Increment" Operation (+1)
;   This depends on what the number looks like right now:
;    * If it's Negative `(P $x)`: "Cancel out" one Predecessor. Result: $x.
;        * Example Rule 1: (neighbor (P $q) ... $q ...)
;    * If it's Zero `Z`: It becomes One (S Z).
;        * Example Rule 2: (neighbor Z ... (S Z) ...)
;    * If it's Positive `(S $x)`: Add another Successor. Result: (S (S $x)).
;        * Example Rule 3: (neighbor (S $q) ... (S (S $q)) ...)

;   3. The "Decrement" Operation (-1)
;   Symmetric to Increment:
;    * If Positive `(S $x)`: Cancel out one Successor. Result: $x.
;        * Rule 16: (neighbor (S $q) ... $q ...)
;    * If Zero `Z`: Become Negative One (P Z).
;        * Rule 17: (neighbor Z ... (P Z) ...)
;    * If Negative `(P $x)`: Add another Predecessor. Result: (P (P $x)).
;        * Rule 18: (neighbor (P $q) ... (P (P $q)) ...)

;   Summary of the 30 Rules
;   The 30 rules are simply the 6 Directions $\times$ the necessary Structural Patterns.

;    * Directions that change 1 coordinate: Need 3 rules (Inc/Dec logic for one var, pass-through for the other). There are 4 such directions. $4 \times 3 = 12$ rules.
;    * Directions that change 2 coordinates: Need 9 rules (Cross product of Inc/Dec logic for both vars). There are 2 such directions. $2 \times 9 = 18$ rules.

;   $12 + 18 = 30$ rules.

;   Didactic Takeaway: This demonstrates Partial Evaluation. We specialized the general concept of "Vector Addition" for the specific case of "Unit Vectors on a Peano Term", resulting in a constant-time, constant-memory rewriting system
;   that handles an infinite grid.
